WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.000
Good morning Hank, it's Friday

00:00:01.000 --> 00:00:05.900
Today I share with you a story about guns, indoor tennis courts, guillotines, humorous outfits

00:00:05.900 --> 00:00:08.060
and competing historical narratives.That's right!

00:00:08.060 --> 00:00:10.440
It's time to learn about:
the French Revolution

00:00:10.440 --> 00:00:15.420
Okay so quick background. This is France. France is in Europe. French people like to eat food.

00:00:15.420 --> 00:00:18.720
And one other thing in the 18th century there was this movement called the Enlightenment

00:00:18.720 --> 00:00:21.640
wherein people, including French people like Jean-Jaques Rousseau and Voltaire,

00:00:21.640 --> 00:00:26.540
were starting to argue that rational thought should be the source of truth and authority

00:00:26.540 --> 00:00:29.260
instead of the interpreted will of an omnipotent god.

00:00:29.260 --> 00:00:31.540
That was a slightly troubling thought to the French monarchy

00:00:31.540 --> 00:00:35.680
which, after all, derived its authority and therefore its ability to purchase humorous outfits

00:00:35.680 --> 00:00:38.920
from the supposed will of that aforementioned omnipotent god.

00:00:38.920 --> 00:00:41.900
Okay so it's 1789, the US has just won its revolutionary war

00:00:41.900 --> 00:00:44.600
which, by the way, would've been impossible without the support of France.

00:00:44.600 --> 00:00:48.120
Meanwhile, France has racked up tremendous debts supporting the American Revolution

00:00:48.120 --> 00:00:51.960
and also fighting the Seven Years' War.
Like, to give you a sense of France's debt,

00:00:51.960 --> 00:00:54.780
a lot of people in the US worry about our national debt,

00:00:54.780 --> 00:01:00.320
but in 2009 the US spent 5% of its federal budget servicing its debt.

00:01:00.320 --> 00:01:05.040
In 1789, France spent 50% of its federal budget servicing its debt.

00:01:05.040 --> 00:01:09.900
So France was completely broke, but the King of France, Louis XVI, was not broke.

00:01:09.900 --> 00:01:13.300
As evidenced by his well-fed physique and fancy footwear.

00:01:13.300 --> 00:01:18.460
Louis XVI and his wife Marie Antoinette had a pretty comfortable life in the rather nice palace of Versailles,

00:01:18.460 --> 00:01:22.420
which is the kind of thing that will annoy you if you are a peasant, who is starving.

00:01:22.420 --> 00:01:25.800
As I mentioned earlier, French people, like a lot of people, enjoy eating.

00:01:25.800 --> 00:01:29.240
So in 1789, in addition to France's debt crisis, there was a famine:

00:01:29.240 --> 00:01:33.480
the price of bread rose 50% in one year but wages didn't go up at all.

00:01:33.480 --> 00:01:34.680
So the peasants are hungry,

00:01:34.680 --> 00:01:38.980
the intellectuals are beginning to question whether power should be centred in the Church and the monarchy,

00:01:38.980 --> 00:01:42.600
and the nobility is dithering about, failing to make meaningful financial reforms.

00:01:42.600 --> 00:01:49.120
So in May of 1789, Louis XVI, recognising the crisis, calls the Convocation of the Estates General,

00:01:49.120 --> 00:01:51.480
a meeting of the three estates of France.

00:01:51.480 --> 00:01:56.860
The first estate is the clergy, the second estate the nobility, and the third estate everyone else.

00:01:56.860 --> 00:01:59.360
Which is to say about 98% of the population.

00:01:59.360 --> 00:02:03.440
The third estate is paying all the taxes, producing all the wealth, fighting in all the wars

00:02:03.440 --> 00:02:07.180
Meanwhile, the first estate, the clergy, pay absolutely nothing in taxes.

00:02:07.180 --> 00:02:09.520
I mean, they have to be celibate, but still! No taxes!

00:02:09.520 --> 00:02:13.920
And the second estate, the nobility, spends most of each day playing an increasingly high-stakes game

00:02:13.920 --> 00:02:15.920
of who can develop the most humorous costume.

00:02:15.920 --> 00:02:18.460
So the third estate shows up with about 600 representatives,

00:02:18.460 --> 00:02:21.440
the first and second estates have about 300 representatives each.

00:02:21.440 --> 00:02:25.800
The third estate wants the representatives from all three estates to meet together, in one assembly,

00:02:25.800 --> 00:02:30.620
which would basically become a governing parliament in which the third estate would have a very slight majority.

00:02:30.620 --> 00:02:33.500
But the first and second estate won't agree to this so the third estate is like

00:02:33.500 --> 00:02:38.320
"Fine! You don't want to come to our party? Forget you guys. WE, the representatives of the third estate,

00:02:38.320 --> 00:02:42.020
are now the National Assembly of France! We are your parliament!"

00:02:42.020 --> 00:02:47.040
This does not please King Louis XVI, so when the third estate people leave the room for a break,

00:02:47.040 --> 00:02:53.640
Louis XVI locks the doors and says "Ah! Sorry! You can't go in there! And if you can't assemble...

00:02:53.640 --> 00:02:55.360
You can't be an assembly."

00:02:55.360 --> 00:03:01.720
At which point the third estate representatives were like: "Hmmmmm. I think we may have a solution to this problem."

00:03:01.720 --> 00:03:05.600
The third estate representatives, surprisingly, are able to find a different room in France,

00:03:05.600 --> 00:03:07.440
an indoor tennis court at Versailles.

00:03:07.440 --> 00:03:11.300
And in that room, the new National Assembly swears the famous Tennis Court Oath

00:03:11.300 --> 00:03:15.760
in which they agree that they will not stop until they have written a French constitution.

00:03:15.760 --> 00:03:21.080
At this point, Louis XVI sees the writing on the wall, he backs down, says "Okay! You guys can be a National Assembly."

00:03:21.080 --> 00:03:27.220
But! At the same time, Louis XVI is flooding the city of Paris with troops who are loyal to him.

00:03:27.220 --> 00:03:31.940
So people naturally start to think that Louis XVI might intend to destroy the National Assembly by force

00:03:31.940 --> 00:03:34.960
at which point the revolutionaries get a bright idea.

00:03:34.960 --> 00:03:41.120
So on 14 July 1789 the revolutionaries storm the Bastille prison, not so much because they wanted to

00:03:41.120 --> 00:03:45.020
free the 7 prisoners who remained there, but because they wanted guns.

00:03:45.020 --> 00:03:49.360
And there were a lot of them in the Bastille. And this marked a big turning point in the French Revolution:

00:03:49.360 --> 00:03:54.120
the governor of the Bastille negotiated a ceasefire which saved a lot of lives on both sides.

00:03:54.120 --> 00:03:58.560
And now would've been a great time for everyone to say "You know what? If we start shooting at each other,

00:03:58.560 --> 00:04:02.720
this is probably gonna get worse." But instead, they killed the governor of the Bastille,

00:04:02.720 --> 00:04:06.340
decapitated him, put his head on a pike and paraded it around.

00:04:06.340 --> 00:04:10.560
The next month, the National Assembly released the Declaration of the Rights of Men and of the Citizen,

00:04:10.560 --> 00:04:13.040
the French equivalent to the American Declaration of Independence.

00:04:13.040 --> 00:04:17.360
The declaration declared that all men - sorry ladies - had the right to liberty, property and security;

00:04:17.360 --> 00:04:21.960
rights that the French Revolution, in the end, would do an exceptionally poor job of protecting.

00:04:21.960 --> 00:04:27.620
Meanwhile, back in Versailles, Louis XVI was still King of France, and it was looking like France might be a constitutional monarchy.

00:04:27.620 --> 00:04:30.260
Which might have meant that the royal family could hang on to their awesome house,

00:04:30.540 --> 00:04:37.540
but then; in October of 1789, a rumour started that Marie-Antoinette was hoarding grains somewhere inside the palace.

00:04:37.540 --> 00:04:42.720
And in what became known as the Women's March, a bunch of armed peasant women stormed the palace

00:04:43.100 --> 00:04:48.300
and demanded that Louis and Marie-Antoinette move from Versailles to Paris.

00:04:48.300 --> 00:04:51.040
Which they did, because everyone is afraid of armed peasant women.

00:04:51.060 --> 00:04:57.060
So between May and October of 1789, France went from a stable, if somewhat broke monarchy,

00:04:57.060 --> 00:05:02.660
to a country where a bunch of armed peasant women could functionally put the king under house arrest.

00:05:02.660 --> 00:05:06.560
A country where no one quite knew who was running the show and everyone had guns.

00:05:06.560 --> 00:05:11.280
This problem will soon get better, and then worse, and then a little bit better, and then MUCH worse,

00:05:11.280 --> 00:05:15.740
and then better, and then NAPOLEONICALLY worse, so stay tuned for that next time.

00:05:15.740 --> 00:05:18.540
Hank! I look forward to you teaching me something on Monday.

